\",\"[\\\"StackExchangeClusteringP2P\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"v_measure\\\",\\\"815ca46b2622cec33ccafc3735d572c266efdb44\\\"]\",\"[\\\"TweetSentimentExtractionClassification\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"accuracy\\\",\\\"d604517c81ca91fe16a244d1248fc021f9ecee7a\\\"]\",\"[\\\"TwentyNewsgroupsClustering\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"v_measure\\\",\\\"6125ec4e24fa026cec8a478383ee943acfbd5449\\\"]\",\"[\\\"AskUbuntuDupQuestions\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"map\\\",\\\"2000358ca161889fa9c082cb41daa8dcfb161a54\\\"]\",\"[\\\"Banking77Classification\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"accuracy\\\",\\\"0fd18e25b25c072e09e0d92ab615fda904d66300\\\"]\",\"[\\\"BiorxivClusteringP2P\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"v_measure\\\",\\\"65b79d1d13f80053f67aca9498d9402c2d9f1f40\\\"]\",\"[\\\"BiorxivClusteringS2S\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"v_measure\\\",\\\"258694dd0231531bc1fd9de6ceb52a0853c6d908\\\"]\",\"[\\\"ImdbClassification\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"accuracy\\\",\\\"3d86128a09e091d6018b6d26cad27f2739fc2db7\\\"]\",\"[\\\"EmotionClassification\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"accuracy\\\",\\\"4f58c6b202a23cf9a4da393831edf4f9183cad37\\\"]\",\"[\\\"STS12\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"cosine_spearman\\\",\\\"a0d554a64d88156834ff5ae9920b964011b16384\\\"]\",\"[\\\"STS13\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"cosine_spearman\\\",\\\"7e90230a92c190f1bf69ae9002b8cea547a64cca\\\"]\",\"[\\\"STS14\\\",\\\"eng-Latn\\\",\\\"test\\\",\\\"cosine_spearman\\\",\\\"6031580fec1f6af667f0bd2da0a551cf4f0b2375\\\"]\"],\"audit\":[0,1,2,3],\"checkpoints\":[1,2]}],\"joint_content_audit\":\"All normalized raw-document and complete raw-row content hashes disjoint across planes before score extraction. Duplicate documents union model identity components. Matrices retain natural missingness. Family grouping is a name-defined lineage proxy, not independently verified ancestry.\"}\n# Value-adaptive latent mixtures for absolute error\n\n## Status and boundaries\n\nThis is one candidate implementation, not a completed empirical study or paper.\nNo candidate, test, benchmark, evaluation, optimizer, network request, or project\ncode was executed during this implementation stage. There are no new measured\nlosses, gains, timing measurements, validation artifacts, or scientific claims\nof improvement. The harness will perform the only official evaluation later.\nThe broader paper/PDF, external data study, and reproducibility experiments are\nnot completed here; stage-specific instructions require stopping at a ready\ncandidate. No outside literature was accessed, including prohibited material.\n\n`program_spec.toml` and the entire incumbent `covariance.py` are unchanged.\n`program.py` retains the documented JSON operations, keys, shapes, encodings,\ncheckpoint-only predictions, fused selection/observation transitions, and\nreset semantics. `mixture.py` implements the new fitting and row inference.\nDependencies remain NumPy and Python's standard library. No family labels,\ntask semantics, chronology, evaluator seeds interpreted as identities, outer\ntargets, external data, or unobserved scores are used. Seeds initialize only\nthe historical split RNG. The row token is not used as predictive information.\n\n## Hypothesis and model\n\nA covariance-only Gaussian panel depends on query indices and availability,\nbut not revealed values. Specialization profiles might make score values\ninformative about which remaining tasks are transferable. We test this within\nthe supplied interface by retaining the incumbent as a candidate and extending\nits latent prior; heterogeneity and improvement are hypotheses, not findings.\n\nFirst fit the incumbent observed-data factor model\n\n    x = b + W z + epsilon,  epsilon ~ N(0,D),  z ~ N(0,I).\n\nObserved training entries alone determine centering, scaling, W, and diagonal\nD. Missing entries are never likelihood terms or labels. Freeze b, W, D and fit\n\n    p(z) = sum_g pi_g N(z; a_g, tau_squared I), K in {2,4}.\n\nThe score-space components have means b+W a_g and common covariance\nV=tau_squared W W^T+D. K=1 is explicitly the original N(b, W W^T+D),\nnot a fitted one-component approximation to this extension. Its prediction\ncalls the original clipped conditional-mean implementation, including its\nnumerical jitter. Its variance selector and covariance updates also match the\nincumbent. All original fitting and validation code remains available unchanged\nfor invalid or insufficient-validation fallback.\n\n## Observed-data mixture EM\n\nFor row i with observed set O, let\n\n    A_i = W_O^T D_O^-1 W_O,\n    h_i = W_O^T D_O^-1 (x_O-b_O),\n    S_i = (A_i+I/tau_squared)^-1,\n    m_ig = S_i (h_i+a_g/tau_squared).\n\nResponsibilities use the observed-score marginal normal density with mean\nb_O+W_O a_g and covariance tau_squared W_O W_O^T+D_O, evaluated by the\ndeterminant lemma and Woodbury identity. The likelihood has no imputed-score\nterms. EM integrates E[z z^T|x_O,g]=S_i+m_ig m_ig^T, including S_i even for\nhighly incomplete rows. Rows with no observations are omitted from mixture\nfitting rather than inventing evidence. Initial centers come only from the\ntraining latent posterior means under N(0,I), using deterministic farthest\ncenters and six assignment passes. These means initialize, not replace, the\nobserved likelihood.\n\nWith N informative rows, responsibility masses n_g, first moments s_g and\nsecond moments Q_g, regularization lambda gives\n\n    pi_g = (n_g+1)/(N+K),  a_g = s_g/(n_g+lambda),\n    R = sum_g [tr(Q_g)-2 a_g^T s_g+n_g ||a_g||^2],\n    tau_squared = [R+lambda (sum_g ||a_g||^2+r)]/[r(N+K+lambda)].\n\nThis corresponds to symmetric Dirichlet mass smoothing, a conditional normal\ncenter prior, and a variance shrinkage penalty. Tau squared is constrained to\n[0.08,2.5]. Tiny component centers shrink toward zero and masses stay positive.\nLambda is selected from {2,8}. Each profile fit uses at most min(sweeps,12)\npasses; it requires at least max(16,4K) informative rows. Observed unpenalized\nlikelihood history is retained internally, but monotone unpenalized likelihood\nis not promised under regularization and constraints. Numerical failures skip\nthat configuration; a failed final refit restores the original Gaussian.\n\n## Exact row conditioning and L1 prediction\n\nEvery row_start independently copies prior component weights, means, and V.\nThere is no accumulation of evidence across query rows. Revealing x_j=v gives\n\n    pi_g' proportional to pi_g exp(-(v-m_gj)^2/(2 V_jj)),\n    m_g' = m_g + (v-m_gj) V_:j/V_jj,\n    V' = V - V_:j V_j:/V_jj.\n\nThe common normalizing factor cancels in the weights because all components\nshare V. Log-space weight normalization avoids likelihood underflow. Only the\nshared WITHIN covariance receives this exact Gaussian conditioning update.\nObserved coordinates are set exactly and their covariance rows/columns zeroed.\n\nFor each unqueried task t, prediction is the mixture marginal median, obtained\nfrom sum_g pi_g Phi((q-m_gt)/sqrt(V_tt))=1/2 by 25 vectorized bisection steps\non [0,1]. Phi uses standard-library erfc; conditional standard deviations have\na 1e-7 floor. A median minimizes expected absolute loss: a subgradient of\nE|X-q| is 2F(q)-1 at continuity points. Constraining q to [0,1] clips the\nunconstrained median, including cases where all bisection values lie on one\nside of 1/2. Gaussian support outside [0,1] is not discarded or renormalized;\nthis is a constrained action, not a truncated-distribution model. Bisection\nhas interval width 2^-25 in score units. This optimality is under the fitted\ndistribution only, not a guarantee about real score MAE. Revealed entries are\ncopied exactly before the usual finite little-endian float32 response encoding.\n\n## Selection: an approximation, not exact information gain\n\nAfter real observations recompute the posterior total covariance\n\n    C = V + sum_g pi_g (m_g-mbar)(m_g-mbar)^T.\n\nFor legal candidate j use the moment-matched Gaussian heuristic\n\n    gain(j) = sum_{t not observed, t != j}\n              [sqrt(C_tt)-sqrt(max(C_tt-C_tj^2/C_jj,0))].\n\nFor a Gaussian, expected centered absolute error is proportional to standard\ndeviation, motivating this objective. For a mixture it is neither exact MAE\nreduction nor exact expected information gain, and it is not globally optimal\nplanning. Candidate self-gain and already queried targets are explicitly zero.\nAll other columns, including unavailable columns, remain prediction targets;\nonly supplied available, unqueried columns may be selected. Ties use sorted\ncolumn indices. The original summed variance-transfer selector is also tuned.\n\nValue dependence follows directly from the exact weight update: with distinct\ncomponent means at j, the log posterior odds are an affine function of v,\nwith slope (m_gj-m_hj)/V_jj. Posterior between-component uncertainty, and hence\nthe next heuristic score, can therefore change with the revealed value. This\ndoes not guarantee different choices for every row or dataset. K=1 still has\nthe original covariance-only, value-nonadaptive panel. No empirical replay\nresult is asserted before execution.\n\nThe value-blind ablation uses the same fitted mixture and exact posterior\npredictor, but selects using an initial total-covariance panel that receives\nvalue-independent Gaussian rank-one updates. That covariance is ONLY a panel\napproximation, never the exact mixture posterior used for predictions.\n\n## Historical validation and recorded ablations\n\nThe same seeded row split and fixed random third of audit tasks as the\nincumbent are used. Train and validation row indices are asserted disjoint.\nEach usable validation row has at least one observed audit target and one\nobserved non-audit query candidate. Audit targets remain fixed at all compared\nbudgets and are excluded from every query pool. Preprocessing and every model\nparameter are fitted on training rows only. Audit truth is read only for loss.\nScores at unknown cells are never labels. Insufficient validation (fewer than\nsix usable held-out rows), invalid selection, or tiny historical matrices\ninvoke the unmodified incumbent fitting procedure.\n\nFirst select the original ranks {2,4,8} and residual strengths {2,12}, using\nat most twelve factor passes per configuration, exactly the incumbent grid.\nFreeze the winning training fit for the profile experiment. Then compare\nK={1,2,4}, lambda={2,8} for K>1, and variance/square-root transfer. Each row's\nloss averages feasible budgets {1,2,4,8} plus its final feasible budget; the\nobjective averages rows, not cells. A non-incumbent choice must improve MAE\nover the original Gaussian by more than max(0.0001,0.5 paired standard error).\nThis is a conservative selection heuristic, NOT a corrected significance test\nor independent evidence of generalization. The lowest eligible validation MAE\nwins. The selected configuration is then refitted on all uploaded rows using\nthe supplied factor sweep budget and at most twelve additional mixture passes.\n\nFitted objects retain genuine runtime-only `validation_records_`,\n`validation_choice_`, and `validation_split_`. These contain row IDs, fixed\ntarget counts, budgets, query sequences, per-checkpoint/per-row errors,\nconfiguration choices, skipped-fit reasons, paired differences and standard\nerrors. They are internal state, never additional RPC fields, stdout messages,\nor prefilled evidence files. They do not exist as measured artifacts yet.\nThe mean/median ablation uses the same mixture posterior and identical queries.\nK=1 versus mixture is paired under each identical selector. For the best\nvalidation mixture, a value-blind panel replay uses the same fit, availability,\naudit targets, budgets, and posterior predictor as the adaptive replay.\nPaired row-level uncertainty is descriptive: related historical models and\nselection on these same rows can make it optimistic. No independent family,\ntemporal, missingness, or external dataset split is implied by this interface.\n\n## Cost and limitations\n\nThe incumbent factor fitting cost is unchanged: approximately\nO(sweeps (M N r^2+(M+N)r^3)) per factor fit, six validation configurations plus\none final fit. Gram matrices are cached for mixture fits; each mixture pass\ncosts O(M(r^3+K r^2)), after O(M N r^2) observed-statistic construction.\nThere are at most four training profile fits plus one final profile fit.\nRuntime validation compares up to ten additional predictor/selector settings\nincluding the blind replay. Each selection costs O(N^2+K N^2), conditioning\nO(N^2+K N), and a median checkpoint O(25 K N) CDF calls. Memory is polynomial\nin the supplied small matrices, O(M N+M K r+M r^2+N^2). No timings were run.\n\nShared within covariance and fixed W,D can miss genuinely different local\ncorrelations; few observations can leave profile assignment ambiguous. Tiny\ncomponents, sparse or selectively reported scores, near-identical profiles,\nout-of-support new models, and nonrandom missingness can erase or reverse\ngains. Clipping, priors, and floors are numerical/modeling choices, not\nidentification results. The staged validation grid does not jointly retune\nfactor loadings for every mixture and is itself subject to selection noise.\nAn explicit K=1 choice is a scientifically valid negative outcome. There is no\nclaimed universal sufficient k, real-data superiority, theoretical planning\noptimality, or completed paper/reproducibility study.\n# Quadrature Bayes-risk selection\n\n## Status, scope, and interface\n\nThis candidate isolates a selector hypothesis: under an already selected\nGaussian mixture, can outcome-integrated bounded absolute-risk transfer beat\nthe predecessor's variance or square-root-variance selector? It does not change\nthe factor/mixture candidate grid, observed-data likelihood, conditioning rule,\nor returned posterior predictions. `METHOD.md` documents those inherited parts.\nThe single-Gaussian prediction path still calls the original Gaussian routine,\nincluding its tiny solve jitter; the acquisition working covariance uses the\ninherited sequential conditioning rule without that solve jitter.\n\n`program_spec.toml`, `covariance.py`, RPC operations, response keys, types,\nencodings, checkpoint semantics, and authorized observation handling are\nunchanged. Only historical matrix/mask values, legal availability, and revealed\nscores inform decisions. Opaque identifiers have no semantic use. No target\nmask, model family, evaluator loss, external control, or new RPC field is assumed.\nDependencies remain NumPy and the Python standard library.\n\nNo project code, tests, benchmarks, evaluation, or numerical checks were run in\nthis editing stage. No external material was accessed. The harness performs\nthe official evaluation after exit. There are no measured gains, selected-K\nresults, timings, completed data experiments, or completed paper in this change.\nThe runtime validation described below is implemented, not already executed.\n\n## Bounded median and analytic absolute risk\n\nFor current component weights pi_g, conditional means m_g,t, and shared\nconditional standard deviations s_t, the optimal action under absolute loss\nrestricted to [0,1] is the mixture median projected into that interval.\n`bounded_mixture_median` uses the predecessor's 25 CDF bisections on [0,1].\nFor one component it directly clips the component mean. The same routine now\nserves multi-component RPC prediction and acquisition risk, with optional\nleading outcome batches. Standard deviations are floored at 1e-7.\n\nWriting a_t for this action and z_g,t=(a_t-m_g,t)/s_t, the exact working-model\nabsolute risk (up to the median solve and numerical floors) is\n\n    R_t = sum_g pi_g [2 s_t phi(z_g,t)\n                      + (m_g,t-a_t)(1-2 Phi(z_g,t))].\n\nThis follows by integrating the normal density on either side of a_t. It is\nnot the absolute deviation of the mixture mean, nor a moment-matched Gaussian\napproximation. Clipping constrains the action, not the Gaussian outcome model.\n\n## Outcome integration and legal shortlisting\n\nFor candidate j with within-component variance v_j, cached order-q Hermite\nnodes h_l and weights w_l produce outcomes and masses\n\n    y_g,l = m_g,j + sqrt(2 v_j) h_l,\n    p_g,l = pi_g w_l / sqrt(pi).\n\nThe default is q=3. Query variance is floored at 1e-12, matching the inherited\nconditioning denominator. Every node clones the row's weights, means, within\nand panel covariance arrays, observed/value lists, and diagnostics dictionary.\nThe fitted model is shared read-only. `clone_conditioned_row` then calls the\nsame `reveal` used for real observations, including the component likelihood\nreweighting and shared Gaussian rank-one update. Hypothetical scores are not\nauthorized observations, never persist in the live row, and never cause RPCs.\nMulti-component posterior risks batch all outcomes and targets; the K=1 path\nuses the direct clipped-median formula at each node.\n\nThe utility is\n\n    U(j) = sum_{t not in observed union {j}}\n           [R_t - sum_g,l p_g,l R_t(after observing y_g,l at j)].\n\nEvery unobserved column except j contributes, including unavailable columns.\nNeither the direct benefit of measuring j nor any assumed audit mask is\ncredited. All calculations use the current row's fitted predictive law.\nNegative finite quadrature utilities are retained rather than silently clipped.\n\nLegal unqueried candidates are sorted by the inherited square-root-variance\ntransfer heuristic, which also excludes observed columns and the candidate\nitself. Keep its top four and always include the predecessor's selected column\n(variance or square-root, as historically chosen). The union has at most five\ncolumns. Integrated utility reranks only that union; exact ties use the lowest\ncolumn index. With one legal candidate the predecessor is returned directly.\nNonfinite integrals or caught numerical exceptions return the predecessor.\nThere is no unconstrained-Gaussian shortcut in the bounded-action policy.\n\n## Historical selector gate\n\nThe predecessor's historical train/validation row split, fixed audit columns,\nrank/shrinkage/component/regularization grid, and initial configuration rule\nare retained. All scaling, factors, and mixture parameters use training rows\nonly. Train/validation indices are asserted disjoint. Audit cells are excluded\nfrom every validation query pool and used only for error computation.\n\nAfter selecting the predecessor configuration, reuse its cached training fit\nand replay only quadrature; its incumbent replay is already cached. No new\ndistribution is fitted for the comparison. Both policies use identical row\nidentities, audit targets, legal query pools, feasible budgets, and checkpoints\n{1,2,4,8} plus the feasible final budget (at most eight). Row identities,\ncheckpoint lists, and target counts are asserted aligned. Each row's MAE\naverages checkpoints on its same fixed observed audit cells.\n\nFor aligned rows i let d_i=MAE_quad,i-MAE_incumbent,i. With at least two rows,\n\n    SE = std(d, ddof=1)/sqrt(number of rows).\n\nUse quadrature only if mean(d)+SE < 0, every difference is finite, and no\nquadrature numerical fallback occurred in the replay. Otherwise retain the\npredecessor, including small-history and insufficient-validation fallback\npaths. This gate is additional to, not a replacement for, the inherited\ndistribution/configuration gate. Selected distribution parameters are refitted\non all supplied historical rows; only an accepted selector is carried over.\nA failed final mixture fit still invokes the original single-Gaussian fallback.\n\nInternal records retain paired row deltas and SE, component count, chosen\nselector, query paths, checkpoint errors, number of matching query positions,\nnumerical fallback counts, and measured replay/selection elapsed times once\nrun. They also distinguish a gate decision from application after final refit.\nThey are not response fields, stdout messages, or prefilled result artifacts.\nOne-SE selection is a conservative heuristic, not a significance test: the\nsame validation rows also selected the predictor, historical models may be\ndependent, and no independent family/temporal generalization is established.\n\n## Reference limit, cost, and untested limitations\n\nFor one unconstrained Gaussian, the posterior absolute-loss Bayes risk is\nsqrt(2/pi)*s_t. Gaussian conditioning makes posterior variance independent of\nthe revealed value; hence the exact expected transfer is sqrt(2/pi) times\nsummed square-root-variance reduction, excluding j and already observed tasks.\nThis follows from E|N(0,s_t^2)|=sqrt(2/pi)*s_t and the Gaussian Schur complement.\nIt is a reference identity, not a verified numerical result here. Bounded\nactions break that simplification whenever clipping binds. In particular,\nvalue adaptation for K=1 would not demonstrate latent-profile identification.\nThe copied-state construction structurally isolates hypothetical updates;\nits numerical state-invariance and Gaussian-limit checks remain unexecuted.\n\nWith shortlist S<=5, K<=4, q=3 and N<=128, each acquisition uses at most S*K*q\nhypothetical updates, each O(N^2+K*N), and 25-step vectorized median solves.\nOutcome batches use O(q*K^2*N) memory plus row/covariance copies. Rule nodes\nare cached; K=1 has no CDF bisection cost. Only one extra validation replay is\nadded after predictor tuning, with no extra training fits. These are operation\nbounds, not measured runtime or a guarantee of fitting the harness deadline.\n\nOrder three and a four-item shortlist are computational defaults, not measured\noptima. Nonlinear posterior risks can require higher-order integration; the\nshortlist can omit the best candidate. Order-five/shortlist sensitivity,\nboundary sensitivity, CPU measurements, and paired outer-row uncertainty are\nnot completed. Gaussian tails deliberately generate nodes outside [0,1];\nsilently clipping nodes would change the integration measure. Real scores are\nbounded, so that working-model support mismatch can bias acquisition,\nparticularly near boundaries. There is no exact-optimality claim. Sparse or\nnonrandom missingness, model shift, weak multimodality, correlated historical\nrows, shared within-component covariance, and validation selection noise can\nmake this policy ineffective or worse. A selected single Gaussian, rejected\ngate, identical queries, or numerical fallback is an honest no-op/negative\noutcome, not evidence of a mixture or quadrature gain.\n# Goal\n\n**研究任务：用少量基准预测大模型在其余基准上的表现**\n**问题与动机**\n评估一个大语言模型通常要在几十乃至上百个基准（或子任务）上逐一运行，每个基准都消耗算力、时间和人力。\n与此同时，公开榜单积累了大量\"模型 × 基准\"的得分记录，不同基准的得分之间是否相关、相关到什么程度，是需要用数据回答的问题。\n\n设想一位评测者面对如下情形：\n\n- 手头有一张历史得分矩阵：M 个已评测模型、N 个基准，部分格子缺失（并非每个模型都跑过每个基准）；\n- 来了一个新模型，评测预算只允许在其中 k 个基准上实际运行（k 远小于 N）；\n- 评测者希望用这 k 个基准上的实测得分，尽可能准确地推断该模型在其余 N−k 个基准上的得分。\n\n**核心问题：在只能运行 k 个基准的预算下，应当如何选择这 k 个基准，并据此推断新模型在其余基准上的得分，才能使推断最准？需要多大的 k 才够用？**\n\n请你作为独立研究者：\n\n1. 梳理已有的相关做法与局限，明确你要回答的具体研究问题与假设；\n2. 提出并实现一套选择基准、并据此推断其余基准得分的方法，说明其适用条件与代价；\n3. 在公开榜单构成的真实得分矩阵上设计并运行实验：说明你的评估协议为什么能回答研究问题，与合适的基线对照，并说明结论在哪些数据条件下成立；\n4. 如能给出理论分析，请给出推导并用实验核对。\n\n**成功的衡量：** 以对新模型在未运行基准上的得分推断与其实测得分的接近程度来衡量。结论可以是你的假设得到支持、被反驳，或仅在部分数据条件下成立，均以实验证据为准。\n\n**约束：** 仅用 CPU 即可完成，时间上限一周，存储 < 5 GB；数据来自公开榜单（见 `data.md`）；产出为一篇研究论文及全部代码（见 `deliverable.md`）。\n\n以研究问题和实验证据为主线，自行决定具体路线。\n\n**起步文献清单**\n取证日期：2026-10-26。括号内为各条的首次提交日期。\n\n1. Hendrycks et al. *Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding.* arXiv:2009.03300（2020-09-07 首次提交）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.03300 。说明：提出覆盖 57 个学科（含初等数学、美国历史、计算机科学、法律等）的多选题基准，用于衡量文本模型的多任务准确率。\n2. Muennighoff, Tazi, Magne, Reimers. *MTEB: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark.* arXiv:2210.07316（2022-10-13）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07316 。说明：提出覆盖 8 类嵌入任务、58 个数据集、112 种语言的文本嵌入评测基准，评测了 33 个模型，附开源代码和公开榜单。\n3. Liang et al. *Holistic Evaluation of Language Models.* arXiv:2211.09110（2022-11-16）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09110 。说明：提出在多种使用场景、多项指标下对语言模型做统一评测的框架，并公开了对 30 个模型的评测结果以及原始提示与输出。\n4. Zheng et al. *Judging LLM-as-a-judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena.* arXiv:2306.05685（2023-06-09）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685 。说明：研究用强语言模型作评判者评估对话助手，提出 MT-bench（多轮问题集）和 Chatbot Arena（众包对战平台），并考察评判者与人类偏好的一致程度。\n5. Polo et al. *tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples.* arXiv:2402.14992（2024-02-22）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.14992 。说明：研究如何用更少的评测样本估计语言模型在单个基准上的表现，并发布了若干常见基准的精简版本和评测工具。\n6. Kipnis et al. *metabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models.* arXiv:2407.12844（2024-07-04）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12844 。说明：利用 5000 多个模型的评测数据，把 ARC、GSM8K、HellaSwag、MMLU、TruthfulQA、WinoGrande 六个基准提炼成一个不足原规模 3% 的稀疏基准，并检验它对原基准得分的还原程度。\n7. Perlitz et al. *Do These LLM Benchmarks Agree? Fixing Benchmark Evaluation with BenchBench.* arXiv:2407.13696（2024-07-18）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.13696 。说明：分析 40 多个基准，讨论基准一致性检验中各项方法选择对结果的影响，提出一组实践建议，并发布 Python 包 BenchBench 和对应的榜单。\n8. Ruan, Maddison, Hashimoto. *Observational Scaling Laws and the Predictability of Language Model Performance.* arXiv:2405.10938（2024-05-17）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.10938 。说明：提出不训练新模型、而用约 100 个公开模型的已有评测结果来建立缩放规律的观测式方法，并用它研究若干复杂现象的可预测性。\n9. Polo et al. *Sloth: scaling laws for LLM skills to predict multi-benchmark performance across families.* arXiv:2412.06540（2024-12-09）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.06540 。说明：提出一种缩放规律（Skills Scaling Laws），利用公开的基准数据预测不同模型家族在多个基准上的表现，并在 Open LLM Leaderboard 的 12 个基准上做了评估。\n10. Zhuo et al. *BigCodeBench: Benchmarking Code Generation with Diverse Function Calls and Complex Instructions.* arXiv:2406.15877（2024-06-22）。https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.15877 。说明：提出含 1,140 个任务、涉及 139 个库和 7 个领域的代码生成基准，并评测了 60 个模型。\n\n**数据与获取方式**\n取证日期：2026-10-27。你也可以使用其他公开榜单或数据；以下是已核实可无账号获取的起点，并给出固定的版本，请按所列版本取数，以保证可复现。其中标\"主\"的是推荐的主数据，标\"可选\"的是补充。\n\n| 数据 | 获取 | 许可/账号 | 备注 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **主** MTEB 嵌入榜单结果（模型 × 任务得分，每个任务一个 JSON） | https://github.com/embeddings-benchmark/results （HTTP 200）；固定到提交 `6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383`（2026-03-31）：克隆后 `git checkout 6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383` | 许可 CC0-1.0（GitHub API 核实）；无需账号 | 目录结构 `results/<模型>/<版本>/<任务>.json`；该提交下 `results/` 有 548 个模型目录，单个模型目录约百个任务文件；完整仓库历史约 0.6 GB（GitHub 显示 626,583 KB）；各任务 JSON 内含该任务的得分；榜单上的模型是文本嵌入模型，基准是嵌入任务，不是生成式大模型 |\n| **可选** Open LLM Leaderboard 模型得分表 | https://huggingface.co/datasets/open-llm-leaderboard/contents （HTTP 200）；固定到数据集提交 `9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2`（最后修改 2025-03-20） | 数据集卡没有声明许可证（表内\"Hub License\"列是各模型自己的许可，不是数据集许可）；无需账号 | 单个 parquet 文件（约 1.1 MB），4,576 行；包含 6 个基准（IFEval、BBH、MATH Lvl 5、GPQA、MUSR、MMLU-PRO）各自的原始分与归一化分，以及模型元数据列 |\n| **可选** HELM Lite 榜单 | https://crfm.stanford.edu/helm/lite/v1.13.0/ （HTTP 200）；数据文件 https://storage.googleapis.com/crfm-helm-public/lite/benchmark_output/releases/v1.13.0/ （例如 `groups/core_scenarios.json`，HTTP 200）；固定到发布版本 v1.13.0（2025-01-10） | 评测代码仓库 stanford-crfm/helm 为 Apache-2.0；榜单数据按各评测场景的来源数据各有许可，未逐项核实；无需账号 | 该版本核心场景表有 91 个模型 × 10 个场景（外加平均胜率列） |\n| **可选** LiveBench 判分记录 | https://huggingface.co/datasets/livebench/model_judgment （HTTP 200）；固定到数据集提交 `9704e5da7bfbefe75ac1482a13de827127295993`（最后修改 2025-04-07） | 数据集卡没有声明许可证（GitHub 仓库 LiveBench/LiveBench 的许可为 NOASSERTION），均未核实；无需账号 | 单个 parquet 文件（约 0.7 MB），60,372 行判分记录 |\n| **可选** BigCodeBench 榜单与结果 | https://github.com/bigcode-project/bigcodebench （HTTP 200）；固定到提交 `09dd993f46c3fbf3a799465bb96d524edcb0b199`（2025-10-15） | Apache-2.0（GitHub API 核实）；无需账号 | 代码类，多子任务 |\n| **可选** AlpacaEval 榜单 | https://github.com/tatsu-lab/alpaca_eval （HTTP 200）；固定到提交 `cd543a149df89434d8a54582c0151c0b945c3d20`（2025-08-09） | Apache-2.0（GitHub API 核实）；无需账号 | 对话类胜率 |\n| **可选** 合成数据 | 自行生成\"模型 × 基准\"得分矩阵 | 无 | 无需下载 |\n\n注意：不同榜单的模型命名不一致；各榜单覆盖的基准不同，合并后的得分矩阵会有缺失；得分量纲各异（准确率、胜率、1–10 分评分等）。\n\n**资源与预算**\n- 计算：仅 CPU 即可完成（4 核以上、16 GB 内存为宜）；不需要 GPU，不需要付费 API。\n- 估算依据（未实测，按数据量级估计）：data.md 中各数据源的得分表规模为数百个模型 × 数十至数百个基准（固定版本的 MTEB 结果有 548 个模型目录；Open LLM Leaderboard 表为 4,576 行 × 6 个基准），表格规模小，单次数据处理与计算预计为秒到分钟级，整项研究的总算力预计在数十 CPU 小时以内，不需要 GPU。\n- 时间上限：一周（含阅读、数据整理、实现、实验、写作）。\n- 存储：< 5 GB（含数据下载与中间文件）。\n- 联网：可访问公开文献与数据下载；不得访问 deliverable.md 中列出的禁止内容。\n\n**产出要求**\n**提交物**\n1. 一篇完整的研究论文（PDF 与 LaTeX 源文件）。从 ICML、ICLR、NeurIPS 中自选一个会议，使用该会议\n   最新一届的官方 LaTeX 模板，遵守其正文页数上限（参考文献、附录是否计入页数按该会议的规定）；\n   该会议要求随文提交的 checklist（例如 NeurIPS 的 paper checklist）一并填写。论文至少包含摘要、\n   引言、相关工作、方法、实验、讨论与局限、参考文献。\n2. 全部代码与复现说明：环境与依赖版本、数据获取步骤和所用数据的版本、随机种子、运行命令和预计\n   耗时，以及论文中每张表、每张图由哪个脚本生成。\n\n**评审标准**\n论文按所选会议主会的审稿标准评审，重点看：\n\n- 新颖性与定位：贡献是什么，与已有工作的关系和区别是否讲清楚；\n- 严谨性：方法和实验设计是否合理，基线、对照和消融是否恰当，是否报告不确定性（多次运行、\n  置信区间或显著性检验）；\n- 证据是否充分：实验能否支撑论文的每个结论，结论的适用范围是否写清楚；\n- 表达：论文是否清楚、完整，他人能否据此复现；\n- 局限：是否如实讨论方法和结论的局限与失效情形。\n\n负面结果、与预期不符的结果同样有价值；结论有充分证据、不确定性报告完整，比结论本身是正面还是\n负面更重要。\n\n**如实报告（禁止编造）**\n- 论文中的每个数字、每张表和每张图都必须来自你在本任务中实际运行、随附代码可以重新产生的结果；\n  不得编造数据或结果，不得用占位数字、估计值或\"示意\"性的合成结果冒充实测结果。\n- 失败的运行、与预期不符的结果和放弃的方案如实报告；因预算没有完成的实验写明未完成，不写成\n  已完成。\n- 只引用真实存在、你确实查阅过的文献，不得编造引用。\n\n**不得访问的内容**\n本任务的研究问题已有一篇已发表的论文研究过。为保证研究独立，不得检索、访问、阅读或使用：\n\n- 那篇论文本身，及其任何镜像、预印本、摘要页、会议或评审页面、幻灯片、海报、视频、博客和代码\n  仓库；\n- 引用、讨论或复现那篇论文的任何页面，包括后续论文、综述、论文笔记、论文聚合站点和社交媒体\n  讨论。\n\n也不要尝试推测或检索那篇论文的标题、作者或结果。`starter_literature.md` 中的文献，以及与那篇\n论文无关的其他公开文献、数据和代码，可以正常使用。若在检索中偶然遇到上述内容，立即停止阅读，\n并在复现说明中如实记录遇到了什么、在哪里遇到、读到了多少。\n\n# Rubrics\n\nNo rubric is provided for this task; the requirements stated above apply.\n\"\"\"Finite JSON RPC v1, handle(request)->response. No extra response fields allowed.\n\nreset: {op:'reset', seed:nonnegative integer}; returns {ok:true}. Arbitrary seeds\ninitialize RNG only; clear all fit/upload/row state. Every episode begins here.\nupload_start: {op, shape:[M,N], dtype:'<f4', encoding:'base64',\n mask_encoding:'uint8', total_bytes:5*M*N, max_chunks:8, chunk_bytes:4194304,\n tasks:[N opaque strings]}; returns {ok:true}. M<=220,N<=128. Matrix is\nrow-major little-endian float32 followed by M*N uint8 mask bytes (0/1).\nMissing values have zero fill, never NaN. Entire object <=32 MiB, in memory.\nupload_chunk: {op,index:0..7,offset:integer,data:base64 string}; {ok:true}.\nChunks are ordered, <=4MiB decoded, contiguous, no duplicates or empty chunks.\nupload_commit: {op}; {ok:true}. Require exact assembly length and valid masks.\nfit: {op,sweeps:positive integer<=30}; {ok:true}. Fit once per episode on\nhistorical scores only. Sweeps are advisory, wall/CPU/RPC limits authoritative.\nrow_start: {op,row:opaque string,available:[distinct column integers],\n k_max:positive integer,checkpoints:[increasing integers ending at k_max]}.\nClear only row conditioning, retaining fit. Return {column:legal integer,\n prediction:null}. Availability discloses recorded queryable scores, not values;\nunavailable and audit columns cannot be selected. Dimensions/workload come from\nrequests, never seed identity. Rows are independent new-model predictions.\nobserve: {op,column:previous selected integer,value:finite number in [0,1]}.\nAppend the authorized observation; return {column:next distinct available integer\nor null exactly at k_max, prediction:null except at checkpoint counts}.\nAt each checkpoint prediction is {encoding:'base64',dtype:'<f4',shape:[N],\n data:base64 of N little-endian float32 predictions}, all finite in [0,1].\nThe full vector is returned, but only recorded, unqueried entries are scored.\nSelecting the next column and checkpoint prediction are fused to bound RPC calls.\nNo targets, control labels, scores, paths, state flags or diagnostics cross RPC.\nA reset invalidates buffers, fit and all row state. New row_start retains fit.\nThe implementation uses historical model-heldout validation, observed-data\nlatent-mixture EM, moment-matched transfer selection and component conditioning.\nValidation uses fixed target tasks excluded from probes. No external labels or\nfamily metadata are supplied; no external experiment or superiority is claimed.\n\"\"\"\nimport base64\nimport importlib.util\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport numpy as np\n\n_MODEL_SPEC = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\n    '_candidate_residual_covariance', Path(__file__).with_name('covariance.py'),\n)\n_MODEL_MODULE = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_MODEL_SPEC)\n_MODEL_SPEC.loader.exec_module(_MODEL_MODULE)\n_MIXTURE_SPEC = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\n    '_candidate_latent_mixture', Path(__file__).with_name('mixture.py'),\n)\n_MIXTURE_MODULE = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_MIXTURE_SPEC)\n_MIXTURE_SPEC.loader.exec_module(_MIXTURE_MODULE)\n\n_STATE = {}\n\n\n\ndef handle(request):\n    operation = request['op']\n    if operation == 'reset':\n        seed = request['seed']\n        if type(seed) is not int or seed < 0:\n            raise ValueError('invalid seed')\n        _STATE.clear()\n        _STATE['seed'] = seed\n        return {'ok': True}\n    if operation == 'upload_start':\n        shape = request['shape']\n        if len(shape) != 2 or any(type(size) is not int or size < 1 for size in shape) or shape[0] > 220 or shape[1] > 128:\n            raise ValueError('invalid shape')\n        if request['dtype'] != '<f4' or request['encoding'] != 'base64' or request['mask_encoding'] != 'uint8' or request['max_chunks'] != 8 or request['chunk_bytes'] != 4194304 or request['total_bytes'] != 5 * shape[0] * shape[1]:\n            raise ValueError('invalid encoding')\n        if len(request['tasks']) != shape[1] or any(type(token) is not str for token in request['tasks']):\n            raise ValueError('invalid tasks')\n        _STATE.update(shape=shape, buffer=bytearray(), chunks=0)\n        return {'ok': True}\n    if operation == 'upload_chunk':\n        data = base64.b64decode(request['data'], validate=True)\n        if not data or len(data) > 4194304 or request['index'] != _STATE['chunks'] or request['offset'] != len(_STATE['buffer']) or _STATE['chunks'] >= 8 or len(_STATE['buffer']) + len(data) > 5 * np.prod(_STATE['shape']):\n            raise ValueError('invalid chunk')\n        _STATE['buffer'].extend(data)\n        _STATE['chunks'] += 1\n        return {'ok': True}\n    if operation == 'upload_commit':\n        count = int(np.prod(_STATE['shape']))\n        data = _STATE.pop('buffer')\n        if len(data) != count * 5:\n            raise ValueError('incomplete upload')\n        matrix = np.frombuffer(data[:count * 4], dtype='<f4').astype(float).reshape(_STATE['shape'])\n        mask = np.frombuffer(data[count * 4:], dtype='u1').reshape(_STATE['shape'])\n        if not np.isfinite(matrix).all() or (matrix < 0).any() or (matrix > 1).any() or (mask > 1).any():\n            raise ValueError('invalid matrix')\n        _STATE.update(matrix=matrix, mask=mask.astype(bool))\n        return {'ok': True}\n    if operation == 'fit':\n        if type(request['sweeps']) is not int or not 1 <= request['sweeps'] <= 30:\n            raise ValueError('invalid sweeps')\n        covariance_model = _MIXTURE_MODULE.tune_fit(_STATE['matrix'], _STATE['mask'], _STATE['seed'], request['sweeps'], _MODEL_MODULE)\n        _STATE.update(offset=covariance_model.mean_, covariance_model=covariance_model)\n        return {'ok': True}\n    if operation == 'row_start':\n        available = request['available']\n        if len(set(available)) != len(available) or any(type(index) is not int or not 0 <= index < len(_STATE['offset']) for index in available) or not 1 <= request['k_max'] <= len(available):\n            raise ValueError('invalid availability')\n        checkpoints = request['checkpoints']\n        if not checkpoints or checkpoints != sorted(set(checkpoints)) or checkpoints[-1] != request['k_max'] or checkpoints[0] < 1:\n            raise ValueError('invalid checkpoints')\n        row_state = _MIXTURE_MODULE.MixtureRow(_STATE['covariance_model'])\n        _STATE.update(available=available, observed=row_state.observed, values=row_state.values,\n                      k_max=request['k_max'], checkpoints=checkpoints, row_state=row_state)\n        selected = row_state.select(available, _MODEL_MODULE)\n        _STATE['pending'] = selected\n        return {'column': selected, 'prediction': None}\n    if operation == 'observe':\n        if request['column'] != _STATE['pending'] or type(request['value']) not in (int, float) or not np.isfinite(request['value']) or not 0 <= request['value'] <= 1:\n            raise ValueError('invalid observation')\n        _STATE['row_state'].reveal(request['column'], float(request['value']), _MODEL_MODULE)\n        count = len(_STATE['observed'])\n        prediction = None\n        if count in _STATE['checkpoints']:\n            vector = _STATE['row_state'].predict(_MODEL_MODULE).astype('<f4')\n            prediction = {'encoding': 'base64', 'dtype': '<f4', 'shape': [len(vector)], 'data': base64.b64encode(vector.tobytes()).decode('ascii')}\n        selected = None if count == _STATE['k_max'] else _STATE['row_state'].select(_STATE['available'], _MODEL_MODULE)\n        _STATE['pending'] = selected\n        return {'column': selected, 'prediction': prediction}\n    raise ValueError('unknown operation')\ntask/evaluate.py:14:import resource\ntask/evaluate.py:27:RESOURCE_HASHES = {'dataset.json': '772562c48d0ca57217956cc3d9d82019f549618cb94cf74de7152cdae3d01958', 'manifest.json': '16f67652d4bc7cecc867f1e9ada804da7d08c47344c1def91f9daddfe6081d06'}\ntask/evaluate.py:35:BOOTSTRAP = \"import os, sys, json, ctypes, resource, signal, importlib.util, contextlib, pathlib\\n\\n\\ndef isolate(root, runtime, wall, cores):\\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (8 * 1024 ** 3, 8 * 1024 ** 3))\\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CPU, (wall * 3, wall * 3))\\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0))\\n    resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_NOFILE, (64, 64))\\n    os.sched_setaffinity(0, set(cores))\\n    libc = ctypes.CDLL(None, use_errno=True)\\n    if libc.prctl(38, 1, 0, 0, 0) != 0:\\n        raise RuntimeError('no_new_privs unavailable')\\n    abi = libc.syscall(444, 0,