{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '880,960p' task/evaluate.py; sed -n '141,178p' ../investigation/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/METHOD.md; sed -n '1,115p' ../investigation/ideas/03_quadrature_bayes_risk_selection_for_latent_benchmark_mixtures/program_1/src/QUADRATURE.md; sed -n '1,100p' ../investigation/ideas/01_target_risk_benchmark_selection_with_residual_covariance/program_1/src/METHOD.md; head -18 memory.md; git status --short 2>/dev/null || true\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '880,960p' task/evaluate.py; sed -n '141,178p' ../investigation/ideas/02_value_adaptive_latent_mixtures_for_absolute_error_benchmark_prediction/program_1/src/METHOD.md; sed -n '1,115p' ../investigation/ideas/03_quadrature_bayes_risk_selection_for_latent_benchmark_mixtures/program_1/src/QUADRATURE.md; sed -n '1,100p' ../investigation/ideas/01_target_risk_benchmark_selection_with_residual_covariance/program_1/src/METHOD.md; head -18 memory.md; git status --short 2>/dev/null || true\"","aggregated_output":"            raise ValueError('vacuous permutation')\n        if not np.array_equal(np.sort(matrix[indices, column]), np.sort(transformed[indices, column])):\n            raise ValueError('permutation marginal mismatch')\n    if len(effective) < 2 or len(set(mappings)) < 2 or np.array_equal(transformed, matrix):\n        raise ValueError('ineffective correlation control')\n    if not np.allclose(means(matrix, mask), means(transformed, mask), rtol=0, atol=1e-12):\n        raise ValueError('constant control changed')\n    return transformed\n\n\ndef row_average(values, families, case):\n    if case == 'family_disjoint':\n        unique = sorted(set(families))\n        family_values = [fixed_weight_score([values[index] for index, family in enumerate(families) if family == selected],\n                                            [1 / families.count(selected)] * families.count(selected)) for selected in unique]\n        return fixed_weight_score(family_values, [1 / len(unique)] * len(unique))\n    return fixed_weight_score(values, [1 / len(values)] * len(values))\n\n\ndef losses(prediction, target, observed, selected, audit):\n    remaining = observed.copy()\n    remaining[list(selected)] = False\n    common = observed.copy()\n    common[:] = False\n    common[list(audit)] = observed[list(audit)]\n    if not remaining.any() or not common.any():\n        raise ValueError('empty fixed target set')\n    return float(np.mean(abs(prediction[remaining] - target[remaining]))), float(np.mean(abs(prediction[common] - target[common])))\n\n\ndef aggregate_rows(per_row, plan):\n    return row_average(per_row, plan['families'], plan['case'])\n\n\ndef native_random(plan, matrix, seed, deadline, sweeps):\n    check = lambda: check_deadline(deadline)\n    offset, factors, ridge = tune_fit(matrix, plan['mask'], seed, sweeps, check)\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    values = []\n    audits = []\n    records = []\n    for row, target in enumerate(plan['targets']):\n        check()\n        available = sorted(set(np.flatnonzero(plan['target_mask'][row]).tolist()) - set(plan['audit']))\n        order = rng.permutation(available).tolist()[:plan['checkpoints'][-1]]\n        checkpoints, common = [], []\n        for count in plan['checkpoints']:\n            selected = order[:count]\n            prediction = condition(offset, factors, ridge, selected, target[selected])\n            loss, audit = losses(prediction, target, plan['target_mask'][row], selected, plan['audit'])\n            checkpoints.append(loss)\n            common.append(audit)\n            records.append({'row': row, 'k': count, 'mae': loss, 'audit_mae': audit})\n        values.append(fixed_weight_score(checkpoints, [1 / len(checkpoints)] * len(checkpoints)))\n        audits.append(fixed_weight_score(common, [1 / len(common)] * len(common)))\n    return {'loss': aggregate_rows(values, plan), 'audit': aggregate_rows(audits, plan), 'records': records}\n\n\ndef native_ridge(plan, matrix, deadline):\n    check = lambda: check_deadline(deadline)\n    check()\n    offset = means(matrix, plan['mask'])\n    centered = (matrix - offset) * plan['mask']\n    columns = matrix.shape[1]\n    correlation = np.zeros((columns, columns))\n    for left in range(columns):\n        check()\n        for right in range(columns):\n            overlap = plan['mask'][:, left] & plan['mask'][:, right]\n            count = int(overlap.sum())\n            if count >= 4:\n                first, second = matrix[overlap, left], matrix[overlap, right]\n                first, second = first - first.mean(), second - second.mean()\n                denominator = np.linalg.norm(first) * np.linalg.norm(second)\n                if denominator > 1e-12:\n                    correlation[left, right] = float(first @ second / denominator) * count / (count + 10)\n    values, audits, records = [], [], []\n    for row, target in enumerate(plan['targets']):\n        available = sorted(set(np.flatnonzero(plan['target_mask'][row]).tolist()) - set(plan['audit']))\n        selected, coverage, checkpoints, common = [], np.zeros(columns), [], []\n        for count in range(1, plan['checkpoints'][-1] + 1):\n## Historical validation and recorded ablations\n\nThe same seeded row split and fixed random third of audit tasks as the\nincumbent are used. Train and validation row indices are asserted disjoint.\nEach usable validation row has at least one observed audit target and one\nobserved non-audit query candidate. Audit targets remain fixed at all compared\nbudgets and are excluded from every query pool. Preprocessing and every model\nparameter are fitted on training rows only. Audit truth is read only for loss.\nScores at unknown cells are never labels. Insufficient validation (fewer than\nsix usable held-out rows), invalid selection, or tiny historical matrices\ninvoke the unmodified incumbent fitting procedure.\n\nFirst select the original ranks {2,4,8} and residual strengths {2,12}, using\nat most twelve factor passes per configuration, exactly the incumbent grid.\nFreeze the winning training fit for the profile experiment. Then compare\nK={1,2,4}, lambda={2,8} for K>1, and variance/square-root transfer. Each row's\nloss averages feasible budgets {1,2,4,8} plus its final feasible budget; the\nobjective averages rows, not cells. A non-incumbent choice must improve MAE\nover the original Gaussian by more than max(0.0001,0.5 paired standard error).\nThis is a conservative selection heuristic, NOT a corrected significance test\nor independent evidence of generalization. The lowest eligible validation MAE\nwins. The selected configuration is then refitted on all uploaded rows using\nthe supplied factor sweep budget and at most twelve additional mixture passes.\n\nFitted objects retain genuine runtime-only `validation_records_`,\n`validation_choice_`, and `validation_split_`. These contain row IDs, fixed\ntarget counts, budgets, query sequences, per-checkpoint/per-row errors,\nconfiguration choices, skipped-fit reasons, paired differences and standard\nerrors. They are internal state, never additional RPC fields, stdout messages,\nor prefilled evidence files. They do not exist as measured artifacts yet.\nThe mean/median ablation uses the same mixture posterior and identical queries.\nK=1 versus mixture is paired under each identical selector. For the best\nvalidation mixture, a value-blind panel replay uses the same fit, availability,\naudit targets, budgets, and posterior predictor as the adaptive replay.\nPaired row-level uncertainty is descriptive: related historical models and\nselection on these same rows can make it optimistic. No independent family,\ntemporal, missingness, or external dataset split is implied by this interface.\n\n# Quadrature Bayes-risk selection\n\n## Status, scope, and interface\n\nThis candidate isolates a selector hypothesis: under an already selected\nGaussian mixture, can outcome-integrated bounded absolute-risk transfer beat\nthe predecessor's variance or square-root-variance selector? It does not change\nthe factor/mixture candidate grid, observed-data likelihood, conditioning rule,\nor returned posterior predictions. `METHOD.md` documents those inherited parts.\nThe single-Gaussian prediction path still calls the original Gaussian routine,\nincluding its tiny solve jitter; the acquisition working covariance uses the\ninherited sequential conditioning rule without that solve jitter.\n\n`program_spec.toml`, `covariance.py`, RPC operations, response keys, types,\nencodings, checkpoint semantics, and authorized observation handling are\nunchanged. Only historical matrix/mask values, legal availability, and revealed\nscores inform decisions. Opaque identifiers have no semantic use. No target\nmask, model family, evaluator loss, external control, or new RPC field is assumed.\nDependencies remain NumPy and the Python standard library.\n\nNo project code, tests, benchmarks, evaluation, or numerical checks were run in\nthis editing stage. No external material was accessed. The harness performs\nthe official evaluation after exit. There are no measured gains, selected-K\nresults, timings, completed data experiments, or completed paper in this change.\nThe runtime validation described below is implemented, not already executed.\n\n## Bounded median and analytic absolute risk\n\nFor current component weights pi_g, conditional means m_g,t, and shared\nconditional standard deviations s_t, the optimal action under absolute loss\nrestricted to [0,1] is the mixture median projected into that interval.\n`bounded_mixture_median` uses the predecessor's 25 CDF bisections on [0,1].\nFor one component it directly clips the component mean. The same routine now\nserves multi-component RPC prediction and acquisition risk, with optional\nleading outcome batches. Standard deviations are floored at 1e-7.\n\nWriting a_t for this action and z_g,t=(a_t-m_g,t)/s_t, the exact working-model\nabsolute risk (up to the median solve and numerical floors) is\n\n    R_t = sum_g pi_g [2 s_t phi(z_g,t)\n                      + (m_g,t-a_t)(1-2 Phi(z_g,t))].\n\nThis follows by integrating the normal density on either side of a_t. It is\nnot the absolute deviation of the mixture mean, nor a moment-matched Gaussian\napproximation. Clipping constrains the action, not the Gaussian outcome model.\n\n## Outcome integration and legal shortlisting\n\nFor candidate j with within-component variance v_j, cached order-q Hermite\nnodes h_l and weights w_l produce outcomes and masses\n\n    y_g,l = m_g,j + sqrt(2 v_j) h_l,\n    p_g,l = pi_g w_l / sqrt(pi).\n\nThe default is q=3. Query variance is floored at 1e-12, matching the inherited\nconditioning denominator. Every node clones the row's weights, means, within\nand panel covariance arrays, observed/value lists, and diagnostics dictionary.\nThe fitted model is shared read-only. `clone_conditioned_row` then calls the\nsame `reveal` used for real observations, including the component likelihood\nreweighting and shared Gaussian rank-one update. Hypothetical scores are not\nauthorized observations, never persist in the live row, and never cause RPCs.\nMulti-component posterior risks batch all outcomes and targets; the K=1 path\nuses the direct clipped-median formula at each node.\n\nThe utility is\n\n    U(j) = sum_{t not in observed union {j}}\n           [R_t - sum_g,l p_g,l R_t(after observing y_g,l at j)].\n\nEvery unobserved column except j contributes, including unavailable columns.\nNeither the direct benefit of measuring j nor any assumed audit mask is\ncredited. All calculations use the current row's fitted predictive law.\nNegative finite quadrature utilities are retained rather than silently clipped.\n\nLegal unqueried candidates are sorted by the inherited square-root-variance\ntransfer heuristic, which also excludes observed columns and the candidate\nitself. Keep its top four and always include the predecessor's selected column\n(variance or square-root, as historically chosen). The union has at most five\ncolumns. Integrated utility reranks only that union; exact ties use the lowest\ncolumn index. With one legal candidate the predecessor is returned directly.\nNonfinite integrals or caught numerical exceptions return the predecessor.\nThere is no unconstrained-Gaussian shortcut in the bounded-action policy.\n\n## Historical selector gate\n\nThe predecessor's historical train/validation row split, fixed audit columns,\nrank/shrinkage/component/regularization grid, and initial configuration rule\nare retained. All scaling, factors, and mixture parameters use training rows\nonly. Train/validation indices are asserted disjoint. Audit cells are excluded\nfrom every validation query pool and used only for error computation.\n\nAfter selecting the predecessor configuration, reuse its cached training fit\nand replay only quadrature; its incumbent replay is already cached. No new\ndistribution is fitted for the comparison. Both policies use identical row\nidentities, audit targets, legal query pools, feasible budgets, and checkpoints\n{1,2,4,8} plus the feasible final budget (at most eight). Row identities,\ncheckpoint lists, and target counts are asserted aligned. Each row's MAE\naverages checkpoints on its same fixed observed audit cells.\n\nFor aligned rows i let d_i=MAE_quad,i-MAE_incumbent,i. With at least two rows,\n\n    SE = std(d, ddof=1)/sqrt(number of rows).\n\nUse quadrature only if mean(d)+SE < 0, every difference is finite, and no\nquadrature numerical fallback occurred in the replay. Otherwise retain the\npredecessor, including small-history and insufficient-validation fallback\npaths. This gate is additional to, not a replacement for, the inherited\ndistribution/configuration gate. Selected distribution parameters are refitted\non all supplied historical rows; only an accepted selector is carried over.\nA failed final mixture fit still invokes the original single-Gaussian fallback.\n\nInternal records retain paired row deltas and SE, component count, chosen\nselector, query paths, checkpoint errors, number of matching query positions,\nnumerical fallback counts, and measured replay/selection elapsed times once\nrun. They also distinguish a gate decision from application after final refit.\n# Target-risk residual covariance candidate\n\nThe RPC and `program_spec.toml` are unchanged. Only NumPy and the Python standard\nlibrary are required, as in the starter. All scores arrive in [0,1]; task names\nare opaque, and no data downloads, family inference or score extraction occurs.\n\n## Fit and prediction\n\nThe historical matrix is split deterministically by rows using the reset seed.\nRanks 2, 4 and 8 (subject to dimensions) and residual prior strengths 2 and 12\nare selected using held-out historical models. Training means and scales use\nobserved training cells only. A fixed random third of tasks supplies validation\ntargets; these tasks cannot be probed, so hyperparameters are compared on the\nsame observed targets. Validation averages model-level MAE over budgets\n1, 2, 4 and 8 when feasible, including the final feasible budget. Afterwards,\nthe chosen model is refitted on all historical rows, never on new query rows.\nVery small historical matrices use a fixed configuration without validation.\n\nThe model is x = mu + L z + epsilon, with z standard Gaussian and independent\nGaussian task residuals of positive diagonal covariance D. Missing-data EM\ncomputes exact latent posterior means and second moments from observed cells,\nthen updates each task's intercept, loading and expected residual error. A\nzero-filled SVD initializes loadings only. Residual variance has a positive\nfloor and a weak prior in standardized coordinates. Transforming back gives\nC = L L^T + D in raw score units. No imputed cells enter the likelihood.\nPrediction is mu_T + C_TO solve(C_OO, x_O - mu_O), with a tiny numerical jitter,\nthen clipped to [0,1]. D enters C_OO once, not as extra measurement noise.\n\n## Selection and Gaussian identity\n\nAfter conditioning on selected tasks, let C denote remaining conditional\ncovariance. Querying j decreases the conditional variance of another task t\nby C_tj^2 / C_jj. The selector sums this over every remaining task except j.\nAvailable candidates use an explicitly zeroed diagonal; unavailable tasks\nremain targets, but can never be probes. Raw-unit covariance makes all target\nweights one; equivalently standardized coordinates would need scale-squared\nweights. Ties choose the smallest index. The conditional covariance update is\nC <- C - C_:j C_j: / C_jj, using a copied column. Every row starts fresh.\nThis is a nested, covariance-only, value-nonadaptive panel conditional on its\neligibility mask, not a deterministic common panel across different masks.\n\nThe identity assumes a correctly specified joint Gaussian and exact revealed\nscores. It is a one-step reduction on the same remaining targets, not a global\noptimizer of changing-complement error: querying j also removes j's own error\nfrom the scored complement. Neither MAE optimality under misspecification nor\nreal-data improvement follows from this identity. Heterogeneous residuals\ndiscourage task-specific variation from being treated as transferable signal.\n\n## Scope and unperformed work\n\nThe API supplies historical scores/masks, opaque task tokens, per-row query\neligibility and authorized revealed values. It supplies no model identifiers,\nfamily labels, chronology, outer split IDs, test target masks, or truth for\nunrevealed entries. Held-out historical row indices above are not external\ntest identities. No unknown cell is a validation label. External availability\nmay encode an audit exclusion, but its cause is not inferred here.\n\nInternal fixed-target validation is implemented; the requested external\nfixed-target audit, matched random/D-optimal controls, ablations, paired\nbootstrap, real-data study, paper and gain-identity experiment are not run or\nclaimed. The harness owns outer test separation and scoring. No evaluator,\ntest, benchmark or optimizer code was inspected or executed. No performance\nresult, universal sufficient budget, missingness-identification result or\nfamily-shift guarantee is asserted. The candidate awaits the official gate.\n\nRuntime scales approximately as sweeps times (M N r^2 + (M+N) r^3) per fit;\nthe small validation grid has six configurations and at most twelve sweeps\nper configuration. Memory is O(M N + M r^2 + N^2), excluding the upload buffer.\nSelection uses O(N^2) per revealed task, and checkpoint predictions solve a\nsystem of size equal to the number of revealed tasks. No runtime was measured.\n# Memory Log\n\n## Context\n## Idea Discoveries\n## Program Evaluations\n## Failures / Regressions\n## Next Hypotheses\n### 2026-11-12T23:44:57.765386+00:00 | step=paper | scope=paper\n- summary: Reference-query planning started using local artifacts only. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Planning inspection supplies no new empirical recommendation.\n- evidence: paper_data.json contains three evaluated candidates, a final evaluation, and no ablation_studies; figures is empty; references.bib and references_provenance.json are absent. Global memory is a symlink to the investigation directory, so its resolved path is used for append-only logging.\n- next_action: Inspect compact final-evaluation summaries, actual candidate source, and local data metadata before selecting 6–10 nonretrieved citation queries.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
